import{_ as a,o as i,c as t,a3 as s}from"./chunks/framework.iKZ1V-y0.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Elastic Goods Script","description":"","frontmatter":{"lang":"en-US","translationSource":{"title":"弹性物资文案","archivedAt":"2026-10-23T14:20:34.857Z","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=9toKm8HaWhotQyZqCEvArv","scope":"page"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"logistics/src/page-9toKm8HaWhotQyZqCEvArv.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-9toKm8HaWhotQyZqCEvArv.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"logistics/src/page-9toKm8HaWhotQyZqCEvArv.md"};function o(r,e,l,c,d,p){return i(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="弹性物资文案" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="elastic-goods-script" tabindex="-1">Elastic Goods Script <a class="header-anchor" href="#elastic-goods-script" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Elastic Goods Script&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>9toKm8HaWhotQyZqCEvArv</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=9toKm8HaWhotQyZqCEvArv" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>This version was revised by Edward Kerman from xyIvneu_&#39;s script.</p><p>(<span class="mark-orange">Orange</span> indicates content needing revision; <span class="mark-red">red</span> indicates content needing addition.)<br> Black Monday, eerily repeated data, mysterious ratios, suspiciously numerous whole hundreds: when we began studying Endfield&#39;s elastic goods, we had no idea how many dark clouds and sparks lay behind the subject. This video explains what actually drives their price changes, and how these rules can maximize your earnings from goods dispatch.</p><p>Skip straight to the conclusions, or join us in revisiting the problem from its beginning.</p><p>The goods-details screen gives your own week&#39;s prices; visiting friends&#39; Dijiang gives each friend&#39;s weekly data too. Combining all goods, we obtained more than thirty thousand prices for 16 goods across 100 players over three weeks.</p><p>Plotting each day&#39;s price distribution reveals a long tail, naturally suggesting a lognormal distribution. If the next day&#39;s price depends only on the preceding day—a Markov process—we can design an oscillating multiplicative random walk. It oscillates as expected, and aggregating players&#39; prices yields long-tailed distributions whose peaks shift left successively.</p><p>Case closed? It was not that simple.</p><p>A linear fit between any two days in a week gives a correlation coefficient. <span class="mark-red">A positive coefficient</span> means positively correlated prices; otherwise they are negatively correlated. Computing every possible pair yields a correlation heatmap. Comparing observation with model prediction, the overall trends match completely,</p><p>yet Monday seems an island, almost unrelated to the following six days. The previous theory had failed.</p><p>As a last-ditch attempt, we expanded the data to eight days—and surprisingly found a very strong correlation between this Monday and next Monday. Make the table bigger, then bigger again. Clear orange diagonals appeared: strong correlations with prices one week and <span class="mark-red">two weeks</span> earlier. Everywhere else was pale and uncorrelated. Prices are therefore determined across weeks.</p><p>Return to the scatterplots behind those correlations. Put this Monday&#39;s price on the horizontal axis and next Monday&#39;s on the vertical. Each point contains the complete information for one weekly transition: the two prices, their ratio and their difference. Plotting all observations together gives an astonishing image. <span class="mark-orange">Countless data points form several straight lines. More excitingly, those lines pass through the same twenty-five bright points</span>. <span class="mark-red">Inspect their coordinates: from 1600 to 2400, every horizontal and vertical coordinate is a whole hundred.</span> These numbers act like tangible hands firmly controlling the pricing rules.</p><p>Examine what makes these lines special. <span class="mark-orange">Point–slope form conveniently describes lines through fixed points.</span> For this line through the origin, moving symbols belonging to the same week onto the same side makes each side exactly a relative deviation. Lines not through the origin can also be defined by ratios of relative deviations. Are those ratios patterned? Draw a second line through the same grid point: their slope ratio is exactly six-fifths, also the ratio between their relative-deviation ratios. For other lines through other grid points, only a few special simple integer ratios occur.</p><p>Enumerating all possibilities and removing duplicate-counting interference leaves only <span class="mark-orange">nine</span> simple integer ratios, all generated by integer coefficients 4,5,6. <span class="mark-orange">We call these three apparently important numbers deviation coefficients.</span> <span class="mark-red">We can finally answer what happens between this Monday and next Monday: each datum is marked by two characteristic numbers, a grid-point price and a deviation coefficient. To generate next Monday&#39;s value, use the two pairs of coefficients—four numbers total—for the two Mondays to construct a line, then read its value at this Monday&#39;s horizontal coordinate.</span></p><p><span class="mark-red">Similar behavior occurs in Tuesday–Sunday scatterplots. Grid points remain whole hundreds and deviation coefficients remain 4,5,6; only grid-point positions change, shifting the lines through them. Thus seven template-price/deviation-coefficient pairs completely describe the seven weekly prices. Compiling all the data, we were surprised to find that the deviation coefficient is identical across all seven days in every case</span>. There are only eight fixed combinations of grid-point prices and deviation coefficient, which we call templates.</p><p>Combining any two templates produces all lines in the scatterplot. Identical templates produce the diagonal. <span class="mark-orange">Templates with identical deviation coefficients produce lines through the origin, while different coefficients produce lines not through it.</span></p><p><span class="mark-red">To summarize, Hypergryph predefined 8 templates, each consisting of a seven-day grid-point-price vector and its deviation coefficient. They represent gradual increase, gradual decrease, decline then rise, rise then decline, small oscillations higher on odd days, small oscillations higher on even days, large oscillations higher on odd days, and large oscillations higher on even days.</span></p><p>For a seven-day price vector, define its relative deviation from a given template. Combining this with the deviation coefficient defines normalized relative deviation. At initialization, each player&#39;s each item receives a normalized-relative-deviation vector. Selecting a target template and calculating backward yields that week&#39;s price vector. Applying all 8 templates yields 8 different price vectors. Your weekly item prices are selected randomly from those eight vectors.</p><p><span class="mark-orange">Note that sometimes the generated price vector contains several [values beyond] that item&#39;s price bounds. A fixed correction value is then generated within a specified interval. We call these shadow data. Discussion of shadow data is omitted here; interested viewers can pause to read.</span></p><p>This concise, well-supported conclusion explains many interesting elastic-goods price phenomena.</p><p>Sometimes an item&#39;s prices are identical in consecutive weeks because the same price set was randomly chosen. In extreme cases, a lucky player gets the same item prices for six consecutive weeks, or all five Wuling goods match across two weeks. When the price templates before and after a weekly change share the same X values, even different players&#39; different goods can have eerily identical ratios of previous-to-next-week prices.</p><p>The intuition everyone has had since beta—high buying prices and low maximum selling prices on Black Monday—also has an underlying logical basis. All templates have middling Monday prices, whereas weekend prices vary high and low.</p><p>Finally, at least in elastic-goods prices, Endministrators have innate differences. Because every account has a finite set of possible prices, its upper and lower elastic-goods price bounds are predetermined, even if the overall differences are small. Some Endministrators are born with lower-priced goods and earn larger margins; others have higher-priced goods and are more often favored by friends. Fortunately, new elastic goods keep arriving with version updates, so everyone has a chance to change their fate in the next version.</p><p>It is, admittedly, an intuitive algorithm. Your starting point was determined upon landing in Valley IV. Yet every time you meet a Stock Redistribution bunny-head and obtain another elastic good, your seemingly fixed fate gains another chance to change. More importantly, even with disappointing personal prices, choosing friends wisely lets anyone earn more Stock Bills than others. Your choices still matter.</p><blockquote><p>Translation note: The orange source sentence about price bounds is grammatically incomplete; the bracketed wording makes that gap explicit.</p></blockquote></div>',1)])])}const m=a(n,[["render",o]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
